## How `-S`/`--smart-case` reaches the regex builder

The flag travels through **five distinct layers**, each in a different file/symbol:

---

### 1. Flag declaration & CLI parsing — `crates/core/flags/defs.rs` · `struct SmartCase` / `impl Flag for SmartCase`

```rust
// defs.rs ~6139
struct SmartCase;

impl Flag for SmartCase {
    fn is_switch(&self) -> bool { true }
    fn name_short(&self) -> Option<u8> { Some(b'S') }   // -S
    fn name_long(&self) -> &'static str { "smart-case" } // --smart-case

    fn update(&self, v: FlagValue, args: &mut LowArgs) -> anyhow::Result<()> {
        assert!(v.unwrap_switch(), "--smart-case flag has no negation");
        args.case = CaseMode::Smart;   // ← writes into LowArgs
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

The generic parser in `crates/core/flags/parse.rs` (`Parser::parse`) looks up the flag by its short character `'S'` or long name `"smart-case"`, then calls `flag.update(FlagValue::Switch(true), args)`. `SmartCase::update` sets `args.case = CaseMode::Smart`.

---

### 2. Low-level argument store — `crates/core/flags/lowargs.rs` · `enum CaseMode` / `struct LowArgs`

```rust
pub(crate) enum CaseMode {
    Sensitive,    // default
    Insensitive,
    Smart,        // set by -S / --smart-case
}

pub(crate) struct LowArgs {
    pub(crate) case: CaseMode,
    // ...
}
```

`LowArgs` is the raw, unvalidated bag of parsed flags. `case` now holds `CaseMode::Smart`.

---

### 3. High-level args → matcher builder — `crates/core/flags/hiargs.rs` · `HiArgs::matcher_rust()`

```rust
// hiargs.rs ~461
fn matcher_rust(&self) -> anyhow::Result<PatternMatcher> {
